# The Stage Door - a doorkeeper, a book, and the last night of a run.
# Written for the library. One person, one procedure, one decision, and nobody is a ghost.

title: The Stage Door
start: start

== start ==
bg backstage
music room_tone
The stage door is a green door in an alley, and the alley is where the smokers
go and where the bins are, and in eleven years you have never once used the
front.
Inside is a desk with a lamp, a telephone that only rings inward, a rack of
keys on hooks, and the book.
The book is a ledger with a line for every person who comes in: name, time,
and who they are here to see. You write it in their presence. That is the
whole rule and it is the only one you have ever needed.
Tonight is the last night. The run closes at eleven and the get-out starts at
midnight and by Tuesday the door will open onto a building with no show in it.
-> the_book

== the_book ==
You turn to tonight's page. The list for a closing night is longer than usual:
the company, the crew, two of the producers, a critic who has been asked to
come and has come, and somebody's mother.
Halfway down, in the hand of the stage manager, one line is not a name:
see me about the understudy - R
You have seen that handwriting on every list for six weeks. You have never
once been told what it is about.
* Look at the call sheet on the board. -> the_board
* Wait. Somebody will come to the door. -> the_door

== the_board ==
The call sheet is pinned by the phone, and it says what call sheets say: half
hour, beginners, curtain. Somebody has drawn a line through the second cover
and written *no* beside it, and then, underneath, in pencil, as if to
themselves: ask J.
You put your finger on the pencil mark. It is not your handwriting and it is
not R's.
-> the_door

== the_door ==
At half an hour the company comes through in the usual order, and you write
them in as they pass, and they say the things they say.
danny: Last one, then.
you: Last one.
The lamp makes the alley outside into a black rectangle with a hand in it
every few minutes. The book fills. At the bottom of the page there is still
the line R wrote, and no name beside it.
Then the bell goes, and it is not the company, and it is not the time the
company comes.
-> she_asks

== she_asks ==
There is a woman in the doorway with a canvas bag held in both hands.
woman: I'm not on the list.
You look at the page. She is not on the list.
woman: I know how it works. I'm not asking you to break it. I'm asking you to
write me in.
you: Who are you here to see?
woman: Nobody. I was in it.
She says it the way somebody says a thing they have practised in the street.
woman: Eleven years ago, in this building, in the other production. I was the
second cover. I never went on. Not once, not for a matinee, not for a warm-up.
I stood in the wings at every performance of that run and I knew every part
and nobody ever saw me do one line of it.
The bag shifts in her hands. Something in it is heavy and flat.
-> the_choice

== the_choice ==
you: Tonight is the last night.
woman: I know. That's why it has to be tonight.
Behind her, the alley. Behind you, the book, open, with a line at the bottom
that the stage manager wrote and never explained.
* Write her in and walk her to the wings. -> let_her_in
* Keep the rule: no name, no entry. -> keep_the_rule
* Take the book and find R. -> find_r

== let_her_in ==
you: Name.
She gives it. You write it, and the time, and in the last column you write
WINGS, which is not a person, and which you have never written before.
You take her through the pass door and along the back of the set. The house is
filling up on the other side of the flats, a sound like weather. You leave her
where the second cover stands, at the corner where you can see the whole stage
and be seen by nobody.
woman: Thank you.
you: Eleven years is a long time to hold a part.
woman: It was never mine.
She takes the thing out of the bag. It is a script, and it is soft as cloth,
and the corners of it are gone.
-> ending_wrote_her

== keep_the_rule ==
you: I can't. Not without a name on the list, and I'm not allowed to put one
there.
woman: You're allowed to write who comes in.
you: I'm allowed to write who is expected.
For a moment she does not move, and then she puts the bag down on the step
outside and stands with her hands empty, which is worse.
woman: I was in it. Eleven years ago. Second cover. I never went on.
you: I'm sorry.
woman: So am I.
She goes back up the alley the way she came, and the bins are the only things
out there, and you close the door and stand in the lamp for a while.
-> ending_the_rule

== find_r ==
You take the book off the desk and go through the pass door with it under your
arm, which is a thing you have never done, and you find R by the prompt corner
with a headset round her neck.
R: What's wrong?
you: There's a woman at the door. She says she was the second cover. Eleven
years ago.
R looks at the book. Then she looks at the line she wrote herself, and at the
blank beside it, and something in her face closes like a door.
R: Second cover in the other production. That was my sister.
you: Your -
R: She covered for me for a whole run and I never missed a night. Nobody ever
saw her do it. Not the company, not the management, not the critics. And she
came tonight, and you left her in the alley.
you: She's still there.
R: No she isn't.
-> ending_r_was_told

== ending_wrote_her ==
The house goes dark the way it does, all at once, and the overture starts, and
for two hours the building is a machine that runs on everybody in it.
You go back to the desk. The book is open where you left it, and the lamp is
on, and the alley outside is a black rectangle with nobody in it.
When the curtain comes down the noise goes on longer than a curtain call and
somebody's mother comes through the pass door crying, and the company comes
out one at a time carrying flowers and cardboard and pieces of the set, and
you write them all out, and the page fills up, and the last line of it is
still the one R wrote.
At midnight the get-out starts and the alley fills with a lorry and six men
and a lot of noise, and by two there is no set left, only the marks on the
floor where it stood.
You take the keys off the hooks and put them in the tin, which is the last
thing you do on a closing night, and you take the book with you, which is not.
At midnight the get-out starts and the alley fills with a lorry and six men
and a lot of noise, and by two there is no set left, only the marks on the
floor where it stood.
You take the keys off the hooks and put them in the tin, which is the last
thing you do on a closing night, and you take the book with you, which is not.
The book goes in the drawer with the others: eleven years of them, one a year,
each one a ledger of everybody who ever came through the green door.
In tonight's there is a line in your own hand that says WINGS where a name
should be, and it is the only lie in the drawer.
You think about that on the way home, and about a part held for eleven years
by somebody nobody ever saw, and about the fact that the rule was never about
the book. The book was just how you kept the rule.
-> END

== ending_the_rule ==
The house goes dark the way it does, all at once, and the overture starts, and
for two hours the building is a machine that runs on everybody in it.
You go back to the desk. The book is open where you left it, and the lamp is
on, and the alley outside is a black rectangle with nobody in it.
When the curtain comes down the noise goes on longer than a curtain call and
somebody's mother comes through the pass door crying, and the company comes
out one at a time carrying flowers and cardboard and pieces of the set, and
you write them all out, and the page fills up, and the last line of it is
still the one R wrote.
At midnight the get-out starts and the alley fills with a lorry and six men
and a lot of noise, and by two there is no set left, only the marks on the
floor where it stood.
You take the keys off the hooks and put them in the tin, which is the last
thing you do on a closing night, and you take the book with you, which is not.
At midnight the get-out starts and the alley fills with a lorry and six men
and a lot of noise, and by two there is no set left, only the marks on the
floor where it stood.
You take the keys off the hooks and put them in the tin, which is the last
thing you do on a closing night, and you take the book with you, which is not.
The book goes in the drawer with the others: eleven years of them, one a year,
each one a ledger of everybody who ever came through the green door.
Tonight's page is complete and correct and every line of it is true.
You think about that on the way home, and about a woman with a canvas bag who
had practised the sentence in the street, and about the difference between a
door and a rule, and you are not sure you would do it again.
-> END

== ending_r_was_told ==
The house goes dark the way it does, all at once, and the overture starts, and
for two hours the building is a machine that runs on everybody in it.
You go back to the desk. The book is open where you left it, and the lamp is
on, and the alley outside is a black rectangle with nobody in it.
