At midnight the get-out starts and the alley fills with a lorry and six men
and a lot of noise, and by two there is no set left, only the marks on the
floor where it stood.
You take the keys off the hooks and put them in the tin, which is the last
thing you do on a closing night, and you take the book with you, which is not.
The book goes in the drawer with the others: eleven years of them, one a year,
each one a ledger of everybody who ever came through the green door.
You told R. R is the only person who could have done anything about it and R
did nothing about it, and that is the shape the night had, and you write it
down the way you write everything down, because that is the job.
Under the last line, in the space where the totals go, you put nothing.
-> END
